# Line endings.
#
# The repository is uniformly LF: 0 of 257 tracked blobs contain CRLF. The
# working tree on this machine is the opposite -- 231 of them do. With
# core.autocrlf=false and no attributes at all, git had no opinion about the
# difference and would commit whatever bytes a tool wrote. That is not
# hypothetical: a session working on the shared-runtime fix had its editing
# tools convert eleven files from LF to CRLF, and the change was caught by an
# implausible diff stat rather than by anything in the repository, because
# there was nothing in the repository to catch it. Three `#!/bin/sh` installers
# were in that set, and no POSIX shell could have executed them.
#
# `text=auto` makes git normalise to LF on commit. Because the repository is
# already uniformly LF this costs nothing and changes no blob -- it only stops
# the next CRLF write from entering.
* text=auto

# The one case where a carriage return changes behaviour rather than just
# bytes. A POSIX shell script with CRLF fails two ways and neither error names
# the cause: run directly, the kernel reads the shebang literally and looks for
# an interpreter called `/bin/sh<CR>`; run as `sh script.sh`, the shebang is
# bypassed and the carriage returns surface later as `$'\r': command not found`
# at some unrelated line. Pinned explicitly so it does not depend on the
# heuristic above.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Pinned to LF because that is what this file already is -- not because Windows
# scripts conventionally use CRLF. They do, and AAW's own .gitattributes says
# so; pinning this one to its current state prevents drift without rewriting a
# working file, and PowerShell reads either.
*.ps1 text eol=lf

# Binary, so git never touches the bytes.
*.zip binary
*.png binary
